## What it is

LBSM, Latent Behavioral Structure in Low-Dimensional Statistical Manifolds, is an experimental statistical machine learning project. It asks whether adaptive, autonomous, sequential systems show structured, low-dimensional organisation in their behaviour even though what we can observe of them is high-dimensional.

The working hypothesis is that the telemetry such systems produce sits on structured low-dimensional manifolds rather than anywhere in the full space.

## What it studies

- whether behavioural regimes cluster naturally in a latent space;
- whether adaptation, such as reinforcement-guided learning, shapes that geometry;
- whether behavioural drift shows up as the manifold moving;
- whether behaviour over time has a compact statistical description;
- whether any of this carries over from the synthetic setup to real adaptive systems.

Phase 1 uses reinforcement-guided synthetic agents because their regimes are known by construction. They are the first controlled test of the idea, not the limit of its scope.

## The work so far

Seven notebooks cover telemetry generation, manifold learning, HMM inference, online anomaly detection, reinforcement-learning behavioural evolution, manifold visualisation, and a robustness analysis over a 160-configuration grid. The exported datasets and a PDF report for each notebook are in the repository.

## What it is

My whole Linux setup in one repository, across two machines: an Arch Linux daily driver and a Void Linux install I experiment on. It started as `archbtw` and was renamed `ricebtw` on 21 October 2026.

## The two machines

- **Arch Linux** is the daily driver. Arch and pacman still own the kernel, bootloader, drivers, systemd, networking and PipeWire. Nix and Home Manager own the user environment on top: command-line tools, dev toolchains, shell config and dotfiles. It is Home Manager on Arch, not NixOS.
- **Void Linux** is the experiment. It runs runit and xbps, with no systemd, no Nix and no Home Manager. Everything for it is self-contained in `rice/void-linux/`.

Both run MangoWC with DankMaterialShell. Hyprland was replaced by MangoWC in August 2026, and its configs are kept only as legacy reference.

## What is in it

- A Nix flake that pins nixpkgs-unstable, Home Manager and agenix, with the config split into modules: CLI tools, git, zsh, aliases, dev toolchains, secrets, Neovim and GUI apps.
- Plain backup copies of the desktop dotfiles for both distros: MangoWC and DankMaterialShell settings, keybinds, kitty, rofi and notification config, plus the xbps package lists for Void.
- The full wallpaper pool, about 3.7 GB, committed to GitHub and kept out of the local working tree with a sparse checkout so it does not sit on disk twice.

## Where it stands

The Home Manager work is done through the repo skeleton, CLI tools, git, shell, dev toolchains and Neovim, with GUI apps partly done. Fonts, themes and icons were looked at and deliberately left alone, because colour theming is driven by matugen templates.

## A few snippets

Taken straight from the repository. Nothing private is in them: the agenix keys and the secrets manifest are kept out of the repo.

**The flake.** It pins nixpkgs-unstable, Home Manager and agenix, and builds one Home Manager configuration on top of the existing Arch install (`flake.nix`).

```nix
inputs = {
  nixpkgs.url = "github:nixos/nixpkgs/nixpkgs-unstable";
  home-manager = {
    url = "github:nix-community/home-manager";
    inputs.nixpkgs.follows = "nixpkgs";
  };
  agenix = {
    url = "github:ryantm/agenix";
    inputs.nixpkgs.follows = "nixpkgs";
  };
};

# ...
homeConfigurations."sicmundus" = home-manager.lib.homeManagerConfiguration {
  inherit pkgs;
  modules = [ ./home.nix ];
  extraSpecialArgs = { inherit inputs; };
};
```

**Aliases.** One place for every shell alias, with the package ones wrapping paru and the file ones using eza (`modules/aliases.nix`).

```nix
programs.zsh.shellAliases = {
  # system & package
  update = "paru -Syu --noconfirm";
  install = "paru -S";
  remove = "paru -Rns";
  orphan = "paru -Qtdq";
  cleanorphans = "paru -Rns $(paru -Qtdq)";
  unlock = "sudo rm /var/lib/pacman/db.lck";

  # file management
  ls = "eza --icons --group-directories-first";
  ll = "eza -la --icons --group-directories-first";
  lt = "eza --tree --icons";
  cp = "cp -iv";
  mv = "mv -iv";
  rm = "rm -iv";
};
```

**A decision worth writing down.** Python is left out of the Nix packages on purpose, and the file says why (`modules/dev.nix`).

```nix
# python3 deliberately NOT here: it shadowed /usr/bin/python3 on PATH and
# broke virt-manager (#!/usr/bin/env python3 -- Arch's system python has
# python-gobject installed, Nix's bare one doesn't, so `import gi` failed).
# For actual Python dev work, use direnv per-project (`nix shell nixpkgs#python3`
# or a project flake.nix) instead of a global package -- keeps it scoped, never
# shadows the system interpreter other tools depend on.
```

**The MangoWC root config.** It only sources the small files next to it, one per concern, so each part of the desktop can be changed on its own (`rice/arch-linux/dms/mango/config.conf`).

```conf
# Keep this file source-only. Runtime options belong to conf.d/*.conf

source=./conf.d/animations.conf
source=./conf.d/autostart.conf
source=./conf.d/binds.conf
source=./conf.d/blur.conf
source=./conf.d/borders.conf
source=./conf.d/gaps.conf
source=./conf.d/input-keyboard.conf
source=./conf.d/windowrules.conf
# ...
```
